<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-divergence-theorem" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>The Divergence Theorem</title>

<introduction>
<p>
In <xref ref="section-greens-theorem"/> we saw that the divergence of a
two-dimensional field measures its flux density, and Green's theorem
(in its normal form) converted a flux integral around a closed curve
into a double integral of the divergence. In this section we extend
both ideas to three dimensions: the result is <term>Gauss's divergence
theorem</term>, which converts a flux integral over a closed
surface<mdash/>often a lengthy parametrized computation, as in
<xref ref="section-surface-integrals"/><mdash/>into an ordinary triple
integral.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-divergence-3d">
<title>Divergence in 3D</title>

<p>
Recall from <xref ref="eq-div-def"/> that in 2D the divergence of
<m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j}</m> is
<m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = \partial M/\partial x + \partial
N/\partial y</m>, the flux density of the field at a point: positive
where the gas expands, negative where it is compressed (see
<xref ref="example-divergence-fields"/>). The three-dimensional
definition adds one term.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-divergence-3d">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the vector field
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = M(x,y,z)\,\mathbf{i} + N(x,y,z)\,\mathbf{j}
+ P(x,y,z)\,\mathbf{k}</m>. Then the divergence of <m>\mathbf{F}</m>
is defined as
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-div-3d">
\operatorname{div} \mathbf{F} = \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
= \frac{\partial M}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial N}{\partial y}
+ \frac{\partial P}{\partial z}.
</men>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
If the vector field <m>\mathbf{F}</m> represents the velocity field of
a flowing gas, then the divergence of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> corresponds to
the amount of compression or expansion of the gas at the point
<m>(x,y,z)</m>.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-divergence-3d">
<title>Expanding or Compressing?</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the following velocity fields of a gas, and in each case
determine whether the gas is expanding or compressing.
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>\mathbf{F} = x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\mathbf{F} = -x\,\mathbf{i} - y\,\mathbf{j} - z\,\mathbf{k}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\mathbf{F} = -y\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\mathbf{F} = \big( x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}
\big) / \big( x^2 + y^2 + z^2 \big)^{3/2}</m>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-divergence-3d">
<p>
We calculate the divergence of the given vector fields in each case
using <xref ref="eq-div-3d"/>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Part A.</em>
<m>\mathbf{F} = x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}
\Rightarrow \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3 \gt 0</m>: the gas
is expanding, at the same rate at every point. This radial field is
shown on the left in <xref ref="fig-div3d-fields"/>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Part B.</em>
<m>\mathbf{F} = -x\,\mathbf{i} - y\,\mathbf{j} - z\,\mathbf{k}
\Rightarrow \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = -3 \lt 0</m>: the gas is being
compressed; the field is the reverse of the one in part A, with all
arrows pointing toward the origin.
</p>

<p>
<em>Part C.</em>
<m>\mathbf{F} = -y\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j}
\Rightarrow \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 0 + 0 + 0 = 0</m>: the gas is
neither expanding nor being compressed<mdash/>this field rotates
rigidly about the <m>z</m>-axis, as on the right in
<xref ref="fig-div3d-fields"/>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Part D.</em> Writing <m>\rho^2 = x^2 + y^2 + z^2</m>, a direct
computation with the quotient rule gives
</p>

<me>
\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
= \frac{3\,(x^2+y^2+z^2)^{3/2} - 3\,(x^2+y^2+z^2)^{3/2}}
{(x^2+y^2+z^2)^3} = 0.
</me>

<p>
Again, the gas is neither expanding nor being compressed: even though
the arrows point outward, they weaken with distance at exactly the
rate that preserves volume. (This is the field of a point charge in
electrostatics.) The animation in <xref ref="fig-div3d-video"/> shows
all four fields.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-div3d-video">
<caption>
Four velocity fields and their divergences: expanding
(<m>\nabla\cdot\mathbf{F} = 3</m>), compressing
(<m>\nabla\cdot\mathbf{F} = -3</m>), rotating
(<m>\nabla\cdot\mathbf{F} = 0</m>), and the inverse-square field
(<m>\nabla\cdot\mathbf{F} = 0</m>).
</caption>
